LIKE

LIKE is used for pattern matching.

It is particularly useful when searching text.

There are two important wildcards:

% → any number of characters
_ → exactly one character
Names starting with R
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE name LIKE 'R%';

Result:

Rahul
Rohit
Names ending with a
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE name LIKE '%a';
Names containing "it"
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE name LIKE '%it%';
14. BETWEEN

BETWEEN is used to check whether a value falls within a range.

Marks between 70 and 90
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE marks BETWEEN 70 AND 90;

This includes 70 and 90.

Age between 19 and 21
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE age BETWEEN 19 AND 21;


15. IN

IN is used when you want to check against multiple possible values.

Instead of:

SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city = 'Pune'
OR city = 'Nashik';

You can write:

SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city IN ('Pune', 'Nashik');
Course example
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE course IN ('BCA', 'BSc CS');